# Repository documentation standards

- README must be in **clear, professional English**, specific to the actual product. The goal is to let a stranger understand, run, evaluate, and trust the software in under five minutes.
- Create root `README.md`, `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/DEMO.md`, `docs/SECURITY.md`, `docs/LIMITATIONS.md`, `docs/METRICS.md` (if applicable), `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `SECURITY.md`, `LICENSE` only after confirming licensing choice.
- Recommended README order:
  1. Project title + concise one-line value proposition.
  2. Actual verified CI and license badges only (optional).
  3. One authentic screenshot/GIF near top, if captured from the working app.
  4. "Why it exists" (problem and target users).
  5. Capabilities: shipped features vs clearly marked planned features.
  6. Actual architecture and technology rationale; Mermaid diagram.
  7. Quick start with exact verified commands and `.env.example`.
  8. A 60-second demo workflow with sample input and expected output.
  9. API endpoints or usage examples from implemented routes.
  10. Domain method and evaluation metrics (what was measured, data, limitations).
  11. Testing instructions and latest *verified* results.
  12. Security, privacy, limitations and data provenance.
  13. Documentation links, contribution guide, license, maintenance status.
- Do not add dead links, fabricated reviews, fake coverage percentages, made-up performance values, placeholder live-demo links, fake screenshots or badges for nonexistent CI workflows.
- Only add screenshots when authentic assets actually exist in `docs/screenshots`. Put alt text on each image.
- Prefer a concise top section; detailed technical docs can live in `docs/` with links.
- Never claim "production ready", "compliant", "bias free", "legally verified", or "99% accurate" without sufficient documented evidence.
- Add GitHub About description (under GitHub character limits), suggested topics, pinned repo rationale and release instructions in `docs/GITHUB_METADATA.md`.
- Cross-check every README claim against source files, scripts, tests and actual outputs before finalizing.
